  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="5">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 5</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="5" corresp="#wit-g-a">And [we] forbeodað eornostlice ælcne hæðenscipe.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="5" corresp="#wit-a">⁊ we forbeodað eornnostlice ælcne haðenscype.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="5" corresp="#wit-q">Omnem quoque gentilitatem modis omnibus interdicimus.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="5" corresp="#wit-b">Be hæðenscipe. We forbeodað eornostlice ælcne hæðenscipe.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">Concerning heathen practices. We earnestly forbid all heathen practices.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock">And we earnestly forbid every heathen practice.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">(Von Heidenthum. B) Und wir verbieten ernstlich jedes Heidenthum.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">(Of heathenism. B) And we strictly forbid all heathenism.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">‘we’ was inserted in G in the 16th century. 5 and 5.1 are missing from D. Liebermann compares EGu Prol. 1.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
